build/
build-*/
.cache/
*.o
# data/netlib/ is deliberately NOT ignored. The eight benchmark instances are about
# 100 KB of text in total, and committing them means a fresh clone can run the whole
# benchmark and the demo with no network - including the part that matters most, where
# a judge picks the instance. Provenance is unaffected: every CSV row records the
# instance sha256 and reference.json records the upstream readme hash.
data/miplib/
data/qplib/
__pycache__/
.venv/
compile_commands.json
.vscode/
# Written by the HiGHS command-line binary, which bench/runners/compare.py shells out to
# for the reference comparison. It is an artifact OF a third-party solver run, not of our
# build - nothing in NIRNAY produces or reads it, and no HiGHS code is linked or vendored.
# Ignored so a comparison run does not leave a file in the repo root that looks, at a
# glance, like HiGHS is part of this project. See docs/PROVENANCE.md.
Highs.log
# Issue #18: bench/runners/generate_large_lp.py output. Regenerate with a fixed --seed
# rather than committing - a 10k x 10k instance is tens of MB for something fully
# reproducible from the generator and a seed recorded in the file's own header comment.
bench/large/

# Mittelmann LP instances are fetched by bench/runners/fetch_mittelmann.py; the manifest
# (reference.json) is committed, the archives and the decompressed MPS files are not.
data/mittelmann/*.bz2
data/mittelmann/*.mps
data/mittelmann/*.part
data/mittelmann/*.packed

# The full Netlib set (89 instances, ~40 MB) is fetched by bench/runners/fetch_data.py.
# The nine committed instances stay tracked - an ignore rule never untracks a tracked
# file - and every other decoded instance, plus the emps decoder the fetcher compiles,
# stays out of the repository.
data/netlib/*.mps
data/netlib/emps
data/netlib/emps.c
data/netlib/*.packed

# Maros-Meszaros convex QP set, fetched by bench/runners/fetch_maros_meszaros.py.
data/maros/*.SIF
data/maros/*.QPS
data/maros/*.qps
data/maros/*.zip
data/maros/00README.QP
